            $text(
                '<h2>A journey of the heart</h2><p>Muslims undertake Umrah to recognise Allah as the source of all peace, to turn to Him in repentance and to purify their souls. It is a journey of worship, reflection and renewal: the pilgrim leaves daily life behind, wears the simple garments of Ihram and stands before Allah as one of countless servants, equal and humble.</p><p>The reward is great. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that one Umrah to the next is an expiation for the sins committed between them (Bukhari and Muslim). He also said that an Umrah performed in Ramadan is equal in reward to a Hajj (Bukhari and Muslim). For this reason Ramadan is the busiest and most expensive season, and many families plan years ahead to be in Makkah for its last ten nights.</p><h2>Who can perform Umrah?</h2><p>Any Muslim who has reached maturity, is of sound mind, and can afford the journey without harming the needs of their family may perform Umrah. Children may go with their parents and their Umrah is accepted, although it does not take the place of the one they may perform as adults. Saudi rules on age, visas and travel for women change from time to time, so our team checks the current requirements for every pilgrim before the booking is confirmed.</p><h2>When is the best time to go?</h2>',
